WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 6.2: Filtering and Preferences. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 6.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.450
2, Filtering and Preferences.

3
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.900
In Topic 6.

4
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.550
1 you linked GPOs to OUs.

5
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.600
Now you decide exactly who gets them.

6
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.450
You will see the three gates every GPO
passes.

7
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.500
You will filter a GPO to one group, and
learn why computers still need to read it

8
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.750
since a 2016 security update.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
You will build a WMI filter that only
workstations pass.

10
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:33.450
Then you will meet Group Policy
preferences, map a drive with one, and

11
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.100
target it to a single group.

12
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:40.350
As always, you will see the PowerShell for
each task.

13
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.400
Linking a GPO is only the start.

14
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.250
Think of three gates.

15
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.700
Gate one is the link: the GPO must be
linked to a site, the domain or an OU

16
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.950
above the user or computer.

17
00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.200
Gate two is the security filter: the user
or computer needs two permissions on the

18
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.850
GPO, Read and Apply group policy.

19
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:10.500
Gate three is optional, a WMI filter: a
query that must be true on the computer.

20
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.150
By default every new GPO gives
Authenticated Users both permissions, so

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.400
everyone in scope gets it.

22
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.050
Security filtering means changing that
list.

23
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.500
Here is security filtering in Group Policy
Management.

24
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.750
Select SA1 Student Desktop and look at the
Scope tab.

25
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.200
Under Security Filtering you normally see
Authenticated Users.

26
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.450
Select it and choose Remove.

27
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.100
Then choose Add, type GG-Students, check
the name and select OK.

28
00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:42.750
Now only members of GG-Students have Read
and Apply group policy.

29
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.800
That sounds finished, but it is not.

30
00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.850
When you removed Authenticated Users, the
computers lost their Read permission too.

31
00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.700
The next slide shows why that matters so
much.

32
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:58.550
In June 2016, security update MS16-072
changed one detail.

33
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.000
Windows now reads a user's GPOs with the
computer's account, not the user's.

34
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:09.850
So when dpatel signs in on CLIENT01, the
computer account CLIENT01 dollar must read

35
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.300
the GPO first.

36
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.550
It is not in GG-Students.

37
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.800
If only GG-Students is on the GPO, the
read fails and dpatel gets nothing, with

38
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.050
no clear message on screen.

39
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.300
The fix: give Authenticated Users, or
Domain Computers, Read only.

40
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.750
Read without Apply lets computers read the
GPO but applies it to nobody.

41
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.400
You make that fix on the Delegation tab of
the GPO.

42
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.850
It lists every group and its allowed
permissions.

43
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:45.700
Select Add, type Authenticated Users, and
in the Add Group or User box leave

44
00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.150
Permissions on Read.

45
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.800
Do not choose an edit permission.

46
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.050
Look at the list afterwards.

47
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.700
GG-Students shows Read, from Security
Filtering.

48
00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:58.150
That wording means Read plus Apply group
policy.

49
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.000
Authenticated Users shows plain Read, with
nothing in brackets.

50
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:07.050
That means it can read the GPO but does
not apply it.

51
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.700
Both entries are what you want.

52
00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.150
In PowerShell the same work takes two
commands.

53
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.400
Set-GPPermission with permission level
GpoApply gives GG-Students Read and Apply.

54
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.050
Then Set-GPPermission for Authenticated
Users with GpoRead and the Replace switch.

55
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:27.100
Replace matters: without it, a higher
permission like GpoApply would stay in

56
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.550
place.

57
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.200
Get-GPPermission with All lists every
entry.

58
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.850
Here I use a calculated property to show
the trustee name.

59
00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.700
Authenticated Users has GpoRead,
GG-Students has GpoApply, and domain

60
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.550
controllers can still read.

61
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:45.000
The filter is set and computers can read.

62
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.050
The third gate is the WMI filter.

63
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.300
WMI stands for Windows Management
Instrumentation, and filters are written

64
00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.950
in WQL, the WMI Query Language.

65
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.000
Each computer runs the query on itself.

66
00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.250
True means the GPO applies; false means it
is skipped.

67
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.300
Our query asks Win32_OperatingSystem for
ProductType 1.

68
00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:10.750
ProductType 1 is a workstation like
CLIENT01, 2 is a domain controller like

69
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.600
DC01, and 3 is a member server like SRV01.

70
00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:18.050
A GPO can have only one WMI filter.

71
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.700
Remember the cost: every computer runs the
query at every refresh.

72
00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:27.750
To build the filter, right-click WMI
Filters in Group Policy Management and

73
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.200
choose New.

74
00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.050
Name it Workstations only and add a short
description.

75
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.900
Under Queries select Add.

76
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.750
Leave the namespace as root backslash
CIMv2 and type the query: select star from

77
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Win32_OperatingSystem where ProductType
equals 1.

78
00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.850
Select OK, then Save.

79
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.700
The filter now exists, but nothing uses it
yet.

80
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:54.550
To use it, open the GPO, here SA1
Workstation Logon Message, and on the

81
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.600
Scope tab pick it under WMI Filtering.

82
00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.050
Answer Yes.

83
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:05.300
A typo in a WMI filter makes it false
everywhere, so test the query first.

84
00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.750
On CLIENT01, Get-CimInstance for
Win32_OperatingSystem shows the caption,

85
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.400
Windows 11 Enterprise, and ProductType 1.

86
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:17.250
Then run the exact filter query with the
Query parameter and count the results.

87
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.100
One result means true.

88
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:24.550
On SRV01 the same lines give 0, so the
filter is false there.

89
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.400
The GroupPolicy module has no cmdlet to
create WMI filters, but on DC01 Get-GPO

90
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.850
shows which filter a GPO uses:
Workstations only.

91
00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.300
Now the second half of the topic:
preferences.

92
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.750
In the editor, each configuration has a
Policies folder and a Preferences folder.

93
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.200
Policies enforce.

94
00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:50.850
The user sees the setting greyed out and
cannot change it, and when the GPO stops

95
00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.100
applying, the setting is removed.

96
00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.350
Preferences set a starting value.

97
00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:02.000
The user can change it until the next
refresh writes it again, and when the GPO

98
00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.650
stops applying, the value stays behind.

99
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:06.500
That is called tattooing.

100
00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.950
Use policies for security and preferences
for convenience.

101
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.200
Server+ calls this configuration
management.

102
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.850
Every preference item has an action.

103
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.500
Create adds the item only if it does not
exist yet.

104
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.550
Replace deletes the item and creates it
again, so every setting matches.

105
00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.800
Update changes only the settings you
filled in, and creates the item if it is

106
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.250
missing.

107
00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:33.700
Delete removes it.

108
00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:35.950
Update is the usual choice.

109
00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:43.400
With a drive map set to Update, if a user
deletes drive S, the next sign-in maps it

110
00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:44.850
again.

111
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:51.100
One link to remember: the option Remove
this item when it is no longer applied

112
00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:53.350
switches the action to Replace.

113
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.600
Here is a drive map.

114
00:06:55.850 --> 00:07:00.650
In the editor go to User Configuration,
Preferences, Windows Settings, Drive Maps,

115
00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.900
and choose New, Mapped Drive.

116
00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.750
Set Action to Update.

117
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Location is the UNC path, backslash
backslash SRV01 backslash Shared.

118
00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:14.850
Select Reconnect, and under Label as type
Shared, the name users see in File

119
00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.300
Explorer.

120
00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.750
Under Drive Letter choose Use, and pick S.

121
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.600
Leave Connect as empty.

122
00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:27.850
A password stored in a GPO can be read
from SYSVOL, so Microsoft warns against

123
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.300
it.

124
00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.150
Leave the hide and show options at No
change.

125
00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.200
Every preference item has a Common tab.

126
00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:42.450
Run in logged-on user's security context
makes an item run as the user instead of

127
00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.700
SYSTEM; drive maps already do.

128
00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:51.350
Remove this item when it is no longer
applied undoes the item when it goes out

129
00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:52.800
of scope.

130
00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:57.450
Apply once and do not reapply runs it only
one time.

131
00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:58.900
Then item-level targeting.

132
00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:03.950
Select Targeting to open the Targeting
Editor, choose New Item, Security Group,

133
00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.200
and pick SA1 backslash DL-Shared-Read.

134
00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:10.050
Now only members of that group get drive
S.

135
00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.500
Drive maps are just one kind of
preference.

136
00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:20.950
A Registry item writes a key or a value;
in the lab you write Lab equals SA1 under

137
00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.200
HKEY_CURRENT_USER, Software, Lakeshore,
with Update.

138
00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:29.850
Shortcuts put a link on the desktop or the
Start menu, for example to the college

139
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:31.300
portal.

140
00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:37.150
Local Users and Groups can add a domain
group, like GG-IT, to the local

141
00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.000
Administrators group on servers.

142
00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:40.850
Never put passwords there.

143
00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:45.900
And there are many more: files, folders,
printers, scheduled tasks and services,

144
00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:48.150
all with the same actions.

145
00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.800
PowerShell can do part of this.

146
00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:57.050
New-GPO piped into New-GPLink creates SA1
Drive Maps and links it to OU Lakeshore in

147
00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:58.500
one line.

148
00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.950
Set-GPPrefRegistryValue creates the
registry preference item, with context

149
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:06.600
User, action Update, the key, the value
name and the value.

150
00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:12.450
Drive Maps and item-level targeting have
no cmdlet, so you build those in the

151
00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:13.900
editor.

152
00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:16.150
Then check on the client.

153
00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:22.000
On CLIENT01, signed in as dpatel, net use
shows drive S connected to backslash

154
00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:23.850
backslash SRV01 backslash Shared.

155
00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.500
Here are four mistakes to avoid.

156
00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.550
One: removing Authenticated Users and
nothing else.

157
00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.800
Users then get nothing; add it back with
Read only.

158
00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:40.050
Two: a WMI filter that is never true, from
a typo or the wrong ProductType.

159
00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.700
Test the query with Get-CimInstance first.

160
00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.950
Three: using a preference for something
that must be enforced.

161
00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.000
Users can change preferences, and they
tattoo.

162
00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.050
Four: expecting drive S right after
gpupdate.

163
00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.700
Drive maps apply at sign-in, so sign out
and in again.

164
00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:01.150
New group memberships also need a new
sign-in.

165
00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.000
Time to check yourself.

166
00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:08.850
SA1 Student Desktop is filtered to
GG-Students, and SA1 Drive Maps maps S for

167
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.300
DL-Shared-Read.

168
00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:15.350
First, only GG-Students is left on the
GPO, and students get nothing.

169
00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:16.800
Why?

170
00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:21.450
Second, which ProductType does SRV01
report, and does Workstations only apply

171
00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.900
to it?

172
00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.950
Third, dpatel disconnects drive S by hand.

173
00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.600
What happens at the next sign-in?

174
00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.250
Fourth, alee is not in DL-Shared-Read.

175
00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.700
Does she get drive S, and does she get the
Lab registry value?

176
00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.750
Pause the video and answer all four.

177
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.600
Here are the answers.

178
00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:47.050
One: since MS16-072 the computer reads
user GPOs, and CLIENT01 dollar cannot read

179
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.500
this one.

180
00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:51.950
Give Authenticated Users Read on the
Delegation tab.

181
00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.200
Two: SRV01 reports 3, a member server, so
the filter is false and gpresult shows

182
00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:59.650
Denied, WMI Filter.

183
00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:05.500
Three: drive S comes back, because the
Update action maps it again at each

184
00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:06.950
sign-in.

185
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.200
Four: no drive S, but yes to the registry
value.

186
00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:16.650
Targeting works per item, and only the
drive map is targeted to DL-Shared-Read.

187
00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:19.300
Let's see it in the pod.

188
00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:24.350
On DC01 I open SA1 Student Desktop, remove
Authenticated Users from Security

189
00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.200
Filtering, and add GG-Students.

190
00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:31.650
On the Delegation tab I add Authenticated
Users back with Read only, and

191
00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.100
Get-GPPermission confirms it.

192
00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:39.350
Next I create the WMI filter Workstations
only and pick it for the logon message

193
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:40.800
GPO.

194
00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:47.050
Then I create SA1 Drive Maps on OU
Lakeshore, add drive S with Update, and

195
00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:48.900
target it to DL-Shared-Read.

196
00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:51.550
On CLIENT01, dpatel gets drive S.

197
00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:55.400
alee signs in, and there is no S drive.

198
00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:56.850
To wrap up.

199
00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:03.100
A GPO must pass three gates: the link, the
security filter and an optional WMI

200
00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:04.550
filter.

201
00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.200
When you filter a user GPO, keep Read for
the computers.

202
00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.050
WMI filters cost time, so test the query
first.

203
00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:16.900
Preferences set defaults that users can
change; policies enforce.

204
00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:21.950
Each preference item has an action, a
Common tab and optional targeting.

205
00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.400
In Lab 6.

206
00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:28.450
2, about 75 minutes and practice only, you
recreate the Lab 6.

207
00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:35.100
1 GPOs, filter, build a WMI filter, and
map drive S with a registry item too.
